CAT = "cisImplant"; SEG = "IMP"
Q = [
("Under FDA's classification, metallic bone plates and their screws are made of which group of alloys?",
 'Cobalt chrome, steel, titanium', ['Titanium, nickel and aluminum', 'Stainless steel, nickel, copper', 'Cobalt chrome, carbide, brass'],
 '21 CFR 888.3030 names cobalt-chromium-molybdenum, stainless steel and titanium alloys for metallic bone fixation appliances.'),
('A screw caddy has rows labeled by diameter and length. Why must each screw go back in its own slot?',
 'So the right size is implanted', ['So the count sheet matches', 'So each slot gets sterilant', 'So the caddy drains between rows'],
 'Implants are identified by size and placed by dimension. A screw in the wrong slot can be implanted at the wrong length.'),
('A plate set has separate left and right versions of the same plate. What must the technician check before assembly?',
 "Each plate's orientation", ["Each plate's lot sticker", "Each plate's surface finish", 'The plate count on the lid'],
 'Many anatomic plates are made in left and right forms. The outline lists implant identification by specialty, dimensions and orientation.'),
('According to CDC, how often should a biological indicator be used when a load contains implants?',
 'Every load with an implant', ['Once a week for all loads', 'Once a day for each sterilizer', 'Only after a repair'],
 "CDC's recommendation is to use biologic indicators for every load containing implantable items, and to quarantine implants until the result is known when possible."),
('Under CMS guidance, if immediate-use steam sterilization must be used for an implant, what must be recorded?',
 'Data linking it to the patient', ["The cycle's start time only", "The surgeon's request form", "The implant's list price"],
 "CMS memo S&C 14-44 says the patient's name or unique identifier and any other information needed to link the item processed by IUSS back to the patient must be recorded."),
('What does CDC say about immediate-use (flash) sterilization of implanted devices?',
 'Avoid it unless unavoidable', ['Use it for every implant', 'Use it to save turnover time', 'Use it for any loaner set'],
 'CDC recommends not to flash sterilize implanted surgical devices unless doing so is unavoidable.'),
("Why is a washer cycle chosen from the implant system's IFU rather than a general instrument cycle?",
 'Its settings are validated', ['General cycles run too hot', 'Its cycle is the shortest', 'Implants need a rinse only'],
 'Implant and instrument trays from a vendor are validated with specific washer and sterilizer settings in the IFU. The outline lists washer cycle selection as an implant best practice.'),
('A technician wants to mark a set of scissors with a hand-held vibrating engraver. What is the main risk?',
 'It can crack or weaken the steel', ['It can void the warranty only', 'It can magnetize the jaws', 'It fades after a few cycles'],
 'Mechanical engraving cuts into the surface and can weaken or crack it and leave grooves that hold soil. Laser marking changes the surface color without deep grooves.'),
('When colored identification tape is used, where should it never be placed?',
 'Over a box lock or hinge', ['On the instrument shank', 'On a ring handle near the shank', 'On a straight handle'],
 'Tape on a joint lifts and traps soil and blocks the sterilant. It belongs on a flat part of the shank or handle, in the number of wraps the tape maker allows.'),
('Identification tape on a retractor is lifting at one edge. What should be done?',
 'Strip it and reapply new tape', ['Press it back down with a finger', 'Cover it with a second layer', 'Leave it until it falls off'],
 'Loose tape traps fluid and soil under the edge. It is removed, the residue cleaned off, and new tape applied if the item still needs marking.'),
('What is instrument dipping, as an identification method?',
 'Coating handles in color', ['Soaking blades in stain', 'Etching marks with an acid', 'Etching with a laser'],
 'Dipping coats part of an instrument, usually the ring handles, in a colored polymer. The coating must be checked for cracks and peeling at each inspection.'),
('Under 21 CFR 801.45, which devices must carry their UDI as a permanent mark on the device itself?',
 'Reusables reprocessed between uses', ['Every single-use device on sale', 'Only devices implanted for years', 'Only devices sold within kits'],
 'The UDI rule requires direct marking for a device that is intended to be used more than once and reprocessed before each use, with exceptions for cases where marking is not feasible.'),
("A count sheet lists an instrument by a catalog number that no longer matches the maker's catalog. What is the best way to identify the item?",
 'Cross reference the catalog', ['Use the number on the sheet', 'Ask the OR which item it is', "Copy the tray's last label"],
 "Electronic and printed catalogs let staff cross reference old and new numbers and other makers' equivalents. The outline lists catalog use for identification and cross referencing."),
('Which details does an electronic instrument catalog usually give to identify an item?',
 'Name, size, picture, number', ['Price, vendor and lead time', 'Repair history and owner', 'Name, tray and set location'],
 "A catalog entry ties a catalog number to the instrument's name, dimensions, tip style and a picture, so technicians can match items to the count sheet."),
('What is the purpose of a preventive maintenance schedule for instrument sets?',
 'To service sets before failure', ['To replace sets on a fixed date', 'To repair items after they fail', 'To audit the count sheets yearly'],
 'A PM schedule sends sets for sharpening, alignment and repair on a regular plan so problems are found before they reach a case. The outline groups it with documentation and risk management.'),
('A risk assessment for instrument marking looks at which question?',
 'Whether it harms the device', ['Whether it costs less than tape', 'Whether it lasts a full year', 'Whether it matches set colors'],
 "Marking must not harm the instrument's function or cleaning. The UDI rule also excuses direct marking that would interfere with a device's safety or effectiveness."),
]
